{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Remediation complete — Gerrit change 2983, commit `1cb3e5b500ff30d50d640eb310fc1a8266ef5f32`\n\n### Reproduction, then fix\n\nBefore the change, on this ambient-identity-only host:\n\n```\npython -m pytest tests/scripts/graph/test_doctor.py -p no:randomly -q\n→ 5 failed, 17 passed\n```\n\nEvery failure carried the same message: `doctor repair requires a configured git user.email`,\nwhich is the proximate abort predicted by the root-cause analysis on this ticket.\n\nAfter the change, the same command reports **22 passed**. Combined with the second affected\nmodule, `tests/scripts/graph/test_doctor.py tests/unit/test_doctor_dirty_tree.py` reports\n**36 passed**.\n\n### What changed\n\nA new helper `configure_repo_git_identity(repo, ...)` in `tests/_sandbox_capabilities.py`\nwrites a repo-local `user.email` / `user.name` to a **caller-supplied path**, using a\nsubprocess-local redacted copy of the environment after scrubbing ambient `GIT_CONFIG_*`. The\naffected doctor repair tests now pass `repo_root=tracker`, so `_repair_pause` reads the\nfixture-owned tmp repo's identity rather than the developer's checkout.\n\n### The isolation property is intact\n\nThis was the constraint that made the obvious fix wrong, so it was verified rather than\nasserted:\n\n- `tests/conftest.py` and the body of `scrub_ambient_git_config` are **unchanged**.\n- The 4-file scrub oracle passes: **71 passed**.\n- The helper does not mutate `os.environ`, proven by a dedicated regression test\n  `test_configure_repo_git_identity_does_not_mutate_os_environ`.\n- Helper env-mutation / policy / doctor subset: **40 passed**.\n\nSo the suite still refuses to inherit developer identity; it now supplies its own on the path\nthat `_repair_pause` actually reads.\n\n### A latent instance found beyond the reported scope\n\nSearching repair entrypoints (`rg 'doctor\\.doctor_cli\\(\\[\"--repair|doctor\\.run_repair\\(' tests --glob '*.py'`)\nsurfaced additional unredirected repair call sites in `tests/unit/test_doctor_dirty_tree.py`\ncarrying the same defect. They were failing for the same reason and are fixed by the same\nchange. The ticket as filed named one module; the defect spanned two.\n\n### Full contract\n\n`make verify` → exit 2, **22 residual failures, 0 doctor failures**.\n\n| Class | Count | Owner |\n|---|---|---|\n| pandoc / DC wiki-render | 17 | `dc28` / `9119` |\n| sandbox / SemLock / fcntl | 5 | `0eba` / `985f` |\n| **doctor** | **0** | **was 10 — this ticket** |\n\nThe 10 baseline doctor failures are gone and nothing outside the two known pre-existing\nclasses appeared. One baseline sandbox failure did not reproduce in this run; that is a\nseparate pre-existing instability under `0eba`/`985f`, not an effect of this change.\n\n### Note on why CI never caught this\n\nCI runners carry a global `user.email`, which satisfies the lookup regardless of the scrub. The\ndefect was only ever observable where git identity is supplied ambiently through\n`GIT_CONFIG_*` environment variables and nowhere else — which is precisely what the scrub\nremoves. The fix closes that gap by making the tests hermetic rather than by depending on any\nhost property.\n"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1789720760152528336,"uuid":"d58a54c9-6225-4978-9702-b89e78e1c64c"}